From: LEVY%BESSIE@venus.cis.yale.edu
Subject: The Programmer who Walks

Some observations from someone who started out in BASIC at age 15 
and who's been hacking C professionally for the past six years:

(1) Planning and design are overrated.  Sit down and start typing.
    Then refine.  If there's a coherent solution, it will emerge.
    Your best design strategies will come to you when you're in
    the shower, or lying in bed unable to sleep.

(2) What the user wants and what you can do are not necessarily
    opposing tensions in a project.  The best solution is not a
    compromise, but one that captures the common essense of both, 	
    and leaves aside the trivia.  As the sculptor said, the work of
    art is there already in the marble; your job is to chip away
    the excess.

(3) The programmer is the programmer, and the user is the user. In  
    the final phases of a project, it will probably become
    necessary for the programmer to be a user.  But if the user
    ever starts to think of him/herself as a programmer, and tries
    to advise (or control) the programmer, there will be no end of
    grief.

(4) You can't learn how to be a good lover by reading about it, or  
    by taking a class.  Ditto for programming, though classes and   
    books are a fine place to start.

(5) The best debugging tools are a pencil and paper.

