Date: 29 Dec 93 16:13:50 EST
From: Reilly Jones <70544.1227@compuserve.com>
Subject: PHIL: Consensual Reality

My thanks to Romana, Geoff and others for their interesting observations.  I
would like to address some of what Han Huang posted about the three categories
of absolutism, relativism, and absolute relativism because he has placed me in
Category I of absolutism incorrectly as I will attempt to show.  I agree in
general with him that many postings and elements of Extropian thought seem to
fall into Category III of absolute relativism and I think I can throw some light
onto why this is appearance only.

Let me present three categories (just coincidental Han):

I. "They" - consists of fact.  This is objective reality about which the axiom
"Existence exists" speaks.  Individuals identify non-self in this category,
whether it's a rock or another person.  Facts have no weight or value associated
with them objectively.  The universe or reality really doesn't care whether
humans exist or not.  This disconnect from value frequently makes people think
that Han's Category III is correct because objectively, all choices really are
equal, none of them matter at all to the larger universe.  No meaning arises
here, no purpose, no values.  This is what the scientific method strives to
understand.  This is where rationality has "pure" roots, in evidence and
causality.

II. "I" - consists of value.  This is subjective reality, the totally private
sphere of mental concepts, perceptions and qualia.  Value has roots in genes
that develop homeostatic values for heart rate, breathing, consumption, etc.  It
appears to some people that evolution has given us a teleological gift of
"survive and reproduce."  I, however, cannot see that evolution itself has a
teleology at all since evolution belongs to category I of objective reality
which has no "purpose."  If evolution itself has no teleology, then evolution
does not impart teleology to us.  Purpose resides here and here alone.
Individuals choose a purpose for themselves, no one and nothing does it for
them.  The choosing of a purpose begins with "to be or not to be", if the answer
is "to be" then the next choice is how to be.  Once an individual has chosen a
subjective purpose, the individual shapes values to help achieve the purpose.
Coupled with the genetically built-in values, these subjective values then help
the individual to pursue the chosen purpose.  This is private morality,
individual values, subjective morality (good meaning working towards the
purpose, bad meaning working against the purpose).  No meaning arises here.  An
individual, in subjective private isolation, has no meaning.  Individuals do not
communicate with themselves, they think.  I hold to a biological epistemology, I
look to science to understand the category I objective facts of how the
individual "knows" anything.

III. "We" - consists of fact and value.  This is consensual reality.  This is
where meaning arises as we communicate with each other.  We communicate our
private, individual values and our private, subjective interpretations of our
observations of category I objective facts.  Together, we decide what to
believe, what is true, what is good, how to live or die, what is rational and
what is moral.  This is where mathematics and logic arise, not in objective
reality.  They are mental constructions.  Mathematics and logic are not the
Primum Mobile of reality, they describe reality consensually.  As our consensual
concepts develop (memetic evolution, if you will), math, logic, science,
rationality, morality, aesthetics, politics, economics, and technology all
change and grow more complex (if it's extropic evolution).  None of these will
ever reach an "absolute" ideal or any stopping point, they are all boundless
concepts.  This is where social values reside.  This is the meeting ground
between ojective fact and subjective value and both are thoroughly mixed in
here.

Evaluation of choices is not only a subjective private matter, it is a social,
consensual matter.  Evaluation is built in, some choices are good and some are
bad.  This is consensual morality.  The fact that there is "good" is an absolute
within consensual reality.  It is a moral absolute.  This absolute concept of
"good" does not exist in category I of objective reality.  This is where I
believe Han thinks I place this moral absolute, hence my labelling as an
absolutist.  I think what he really is talking about are people who believe that
objective reality contains value (whether god or Tao or whatever), I am not in
this category.  Consensual rationality is what science is, an agreed upon method
to assign value to observed facts.  There are wide variations of agreement on
what rationality and morality are.

When large groups of people agree on purpose, then rationality and morality help
to achieve that purpose.  I have stated that my purpose (and what I want to be
other people's purpose) is increasing extropy, no Finality.  Those who help me
achieve this purpose I will pronounce morally good and/or rational, those who
hinder me I will pronounce morally bad and/or irrational.  Evil is a superlative
for bad and relates to concepts of the Finality of humanity in whatever form
they take (military MAD doctrines, religious Judgment Days, physicist's
cosmologies).  Excellence is a superlative for good and relates to concepts of
the neverending increase in Extropy and the technological achievement of the
boundless projection of the ego.  For those with similar consensual purpose, our
consensual duty is to pursue excellence and confront evil.  This is not an
absolute, not coercive, it is consensual, it makes sense.  No guilt or sin,
these concepts are coercive.  Tolerance and self-restraint are moral extropic
social values important for the establishment and maintenance of individual
rights and spontaneous order per the chapter and verse in the Principles.

	Han, I hope you will place me in your Category II after reading this; if
you do not see it this way, I have not explained very well.  I will attempt to
rail against both Category III and Category I equally.  Excuse me if I go
overboard on Category III because in my judgment, these adherents have
supplanted Category I adherents as the dominant majority of Western
civilization.  Geoff, I am with you on local hill climbing as opposed to long
jump search in fitness space.  I appreciate that your work isn't associated with
MONDO2000, peace.  When you say:  "I would hold that the primary value for
choosing what goes under the label extropianism should rationally be are the
opinions of the people that run the Extropy Institute and founded the
philosophy. Not what you or I think." I wonder, who whispers in their ear?

