From: freeman@maspar.com (Jay R. Freeman)
Subject: Extropially Correct Welfare

Tim May and the First Extropian Squirrel dialog about child-care and
education:

> Then by all means give [potential welfare recipients] none of
> my money.

  Since the Squirrel is for argument's sake wearing his libertarian
hat -- which fits poorly -- he takes no exception whatsoever to Tim's
decision not to pay for activities insufficiently beneficial to Tim.

>                  In a true free market, it would be up to _parents_
> to contract for schooling, not for entrepreneurs to somehow tap into
> future productivity.

  The Squirrel respectfully disagrees.  Parents do indeed contract for
much schooling, but surely an entrepreneur who can turn a profit from
education via revenue from some source other than parents, may do so.
Furthermore, an Extropian purist, or perhaps a libertarian one, might
argue that since children's education is often of no financial benefit
to parents, parents ought not to pay for it in the first place.

  (The example of education as a potential Extropianly-correct welfare
program is confusing, in that parents have strong non-financial
motives to provide for offspring; however, that's an artifact of the
specific example -- the point does not generally apply to actions with
broad, dilute benefits, such as motivated my original post.  But I
picked the example, so I will stick with it.)

  And the potential profits seem enormous -- the productivity increase
from education should light up any capitalist's eyes with dollar
signs.  It's not like cornering the global market on Glocks, gold or
governments -- we're talking real money here!

  Of course, that no entrepreneurs have tapped this resource does not
mean it's impossible -- perhaps someone will eventually figure out
how.  Yet it is Extropianly depressing that many different governments
have done at least a crude job of providing education and increasing
total productivity thereby, while the profit-based stateless free
market seems to provide no mechanism to do so.

>                                                For example, in the
> school example, a true free market would support the right of
> contract, wherein I could make a deal to pay of the education of, say,
> some ghetto kids and they would then be contractually bound to work
> for me for some number of years.
>
> That this is not allowed--some nonsense about "indentured
> servitude"--is indicative that we are not at all in a free market.

  Contract with whom?  The Squirrel agrees that some persons young
enough to benefit substantially from education are old enough to make
contracts, but doubts that all are.  It is those who aren't, that
provide the cases relevant here.  There is perhaps a meta question,
about what is meant by "contract", and about what entities are capable
of participating in one.

                              -- Jay Freeman, First Extropian Squirrel

