From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Empathy and Courtesy

I applaud Nancy for coining the quite apt, if rather cutesy, phrases
"empathically challenged" and "differently courtesied".  A surfeit
of empathy and courtesy is in many ways as harmful to the individual
as being poor or lacking in intelligence.  On the flip side, there
is no upper limit to how empathic and polite one might be, with
practice, and superiority in these areas might be quite valuable.

In the business world, I'd rank empathy nearly up there with intelligence 
as a necessary skill, and in relationships of trust it's much
more important.  Empathy is the glue that lets us construct webs
of trust.  I, for one, would like to greatly improve myself in 
these areas, just as I would like to boost my intelligence.
A true Promethean understands well the emotions of the people
he deals with, as well as his own.  (BTW, understanding one's
own emotions is not at all as easy as it sounds; many times we
delude themselves into grossly oversimplified explanations for
our own emotional nature).

Many of us libertarians idealize the "the polite society", but I've seen
some extremely vicious behavior in the cryonics and libertarian 
activist communities.  Extropians, being in some ways an intersection 
of those communities, have been remarkably free of those kind of attacks, 
until the last couple weeks.  I'm not singling anybody out here, and I'm 
not pleading completely innocent myself, I just think this is a very 
important problem.  For those of us who want to greatly improve our lives 
and the viability of our shared goals such as anarcho-capitalism and 
longevity, I think we can become much more effective to the extent we 
improve our empathy, courtesy, and other skills involved in getting 
along with each other.

